    "summary": "Acoustic gems and electric fire; standout jams throughout",
    "blurb": "Features a crystal‑clear all‑acoustic set that flows into a high‑energy second half with a blazing The Other One and seamless transitions from Lost Sailor into Terrapin Station. Highlights include Dire Wolf, Ripple, Lazy Lightning > Supplication, and a powerful encore of U.S. Blues.",
    "review": "The October 6, 1980 Warfield show opens with an intimate acoustic set that feels almost like a private living‑room concert—Garcia’s warm strums on Dire Wolf and the sweet vocal harmonies on Cassidy are captured in pristine detail by the soundboard. As the night rolls into the electric second set, the band erupts: a scorching The Other One takes the floor, riding a tight rhythm section that propels the music through a flawless Lost Sailor → Saint Of Circumstance → Terrapin Station sequence. The audience‑recorded gems from this night (notably the Nak700 capture) showcase everything from Lazy Lightning > Supplication to the soaring Ripple and the sing‑along power of U.S. Blues, making this one of the most consistently engaging Warfield performances of the era.\n\nAcross the board, reviewers rave about the crystal‑clear acoustic mix and the charged, house‑mix fidelity of the audience tapes. While a few recordings are partial or face Warfield’s notoriously tricky acoustics, the overall musical flow is praised as “consistently engaging” with every song feeling like a masterclass in Deadcraft. Whether you’re after the pristine acoustic SBD or the electric fire captured by the stellar audience recording, this show delivers both sides of the Dead in top form.",
